 A concave octagon with eight at right angles would have one vertex out of the traditional ratio, having an angle greater than $180^\circ.$  That means we can multiply $180^\circ$ by the number of sides in a polygon and get the sum of the interior angles, which is $(180-2)(8) = 1080^\circ.$

If $n$ of the angles are right angles, then the sum of the remaining angles is $1080^\circ - 90n.$  Since each of these angles is less than $180^\circ,$ $n$ must be at most $12$ since $90 \cdot 13 = 1170^\circ > 1080^\circ.$

